Penalties:

- Overcharging passengers or refusing to honor discounts constitutes a violation.
- Penalties include:
- Fines (amount varies depending on frequency of violation).
- Possible suspension or revocation of tricycle franchise/permit.
- Administrative sanctions imposed by the Taguig Mobility Office.

📌 Why It Matters
- Passenger Protection: Prevents arbitrary fare increases and protects vulnerable groups.
- Driver Accountability: Ensures tricycle operators follow city regulations.
- Urban Mobility Policy: Part of Taguig’s broader effort to regulate local transport and integrate with Metro Manila’s mobility framework.

⚠️ Challenges & Issues
- Implementation gaps: Some drivers fail to display the fare matrix or honor discounts.
- Awareness: Many passengers are unaware of their rights under the ordinance.
- Enforcement: Barangay officials and the Taguig Mobility Office must actively monitor compliance.

Penalties for Violations
1st Offense -   1k fine and warning
2nd Offense -   2k fine and suspension of tricycle franchise/permit for 1 month
3rd Offense -   3k fine and revocation of tricycle franchise/permit

What Counts as a Violation
- Overcharging beyond the ₱30 minimum fare + ₱2/km succeeding rate.
- Refusing to honor 20% discounts for seniors, PWDs, students (and later solo parents under Ordinance No. 133, S. 2025).
- Failure to post the official fare matrix inside the tricycle or TODA terminal.
- Other abusive practices (e.g., refusing passengers without valid reason).

📌 Enforcement
- The Taguig Mobility Office (TMO) and barangay officials are tasked with monitoring compliance.
- Passengers can file complaints with the TMO or their barangay if drivers violate the ordinance.
- TODAs are responsible for ensuring their members follow the fare matrix and ordinance rules



